ATT_Turan said:
So the plugin will first evaluate all formulae using the base values?
e.g. I have an ATK of 50.
I have a sword with +10 from a dynamic parameter;
a helmet with +10%;
and a shield with +15%.
It will be 50 + 10 + (50*.1) + (50*.15)?
I think that's what the goal of the above question was.
Click to expand...
Ohh!
Now I get it.
For X and S params, the plugin will get all the default ones from the engine first: States, Traits etc. Then add the dynamic parameters on top of it. Reference below:
JavaScript:
Alias.Game_Actor_xparam = Game_Actor.prototype.xparam
Game_Actor.prototype.xparam = function(xparamId) {
    const value = Alias.Game_Actor_xparam.call(this, xparamId)
    const dynamicValue = this.getAllDynamicValues("xparam", xparamId)

    return value + dynamicValue
}

Alias.Game_Actor_sparam = Game_Actor.prototype.sparam
Game_Actor.prototype.sparam = function(sparamId) {
    const value = Alias.Game_Actor_sparam.call(this, sparamId)
    const dynamicValue = this.getAllDynamicValues("sparam", sparamId)

    return value + dynamicValue
}
For the regular parameters, it will add the Dynamic values on top of the param plus values. Meaning when the engine checks for the ATK of the battler, these dynamic values will be affected by the paramRate and Buff Rates. I guess on another words, for regular parameters, the dynamic values came before the rates.
JavaScript:
// On my plugin
Alias.Game_Actor_paramPlus = Game_Actor.prototype.paramPlus
Game_Actor.prototype.paramPlus = function(paramId) {
    const value = Alias.Game_Actor_paramPlus.call(this, paramId)
    const dynamicValue = this.getAllDynamicValues("param", paramId)

    return value + dynamicValue
}

// On the default code
Game_BattlerBase.prototype.param = function(paramId) {
    const value =
        this.paramBasePlus(paramId) * // DYNAMIC value is inside this value
        this.paramRate(paramId) *
        this.paramBuffRate(paramId);
    const maxValue = this.paramMax(paramId);
    const minValue = this.paramMin(paramId);
    return Math.round(value.clamp(minValue, maxValue));
};
Guess that is a kind of information that should b e on the help file! Will put that on later!

Eliaquim said:
Hi there!
Not exactly, because is not an issue with the plugin itself, but rather how javascript works on rpg maker code. But there are another ways to achieve what the OP was trying to do. Is best if you try to explain to me what you are trying to do and I see how I can help you.
Click to expand...
I would like to increase/decrease a custom parameter by a given percentage. Example scenario, I would like the state “Broken Bone” to reduce Strength by 25%.
Edit: I was able to get it to work with VS's On Add State/On Erase State notetags with the following:
Code:
<JS On Add State>
this._old = this._old || 0;
this._old += this.cparam(0);
this.addCParam(0, Math.round(this.cparam(0) * -0.25));
</JS On Add State>

<JS On Erase State>
this.addCParam(0, Math.round(this.cparam(0) * -1));
this.addCParam(0, this._old);
</JS On Erase State>
